.SS OBJECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT \f[B]struct\f[R]
#define object \f[B]struct\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Used to visually distinguish objects from regular structs.
.PP
This macro has no functional impact.
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
object o_object
{
    void    (*worked)(int);
    int     value;
};
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJ
.IP
.EX
#define OBJ(OBJECT_NAME, VARIABLE_NAME)
#define obj(object_name, variable_name)
.EE
.PP
Instantiates an object \f[B]on the stack\f[R].
.PP
The variable is a plain object value, so its members are reached with
\f[CR].\f[R].
.PP
Calling \f[CR]OBJ\f[R] automatically invokes the constructor.
.PP
Call \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] on the object when it\(aqs done with, to run its
\f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] and free that destructor\(aqs own trampoline
(see \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R]).
There is no allocation to release for a stack object \- it goes away
with its scope as usual.
.PP
The constructor must be named exactly the same as the object type:
.IP
.EX
object o_test
{
    ...
};

void o_test()
{
    ...
}
.EE
.PP
Constructors may accept parameters:
.IP
.EX
void o_test(int arg, char *arg2)
{
    ...
}
.EE
.PP
Usage:
.IP
.EX
obj (o_test, test) ();
.EE
.PP
With parameters:
.IP
.EX
obj (o_test, test) (42, \(dqtest\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Since the macro expands to an ordinary declaration, qualifiers and
attributes may be written in front of it:
.IP
.EX
const obj (o_test, test) (42);

test.setName(\(dqtest\(dq);
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS NEW
.IP
.EX
#define NEW(OBJECT_NAME, VARIABLE_NAME)
#define new(object_name, variable_name)
.EE
.PP
Instantiates an object \f[B]on the heap\f[R].
.PP
The variable is a pointer to the object, so its members are reached with
\f[CR]\->\f[R].
.PP
The allocation is checked before the constructor runs: if
\f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] fails, the variable is \f[CR]NULL\f[R] and the
constructor is never called.
.PP
Unlike \f[CR]OBJ\f[R], heap instances own their storage.
Call \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] on the object when it\(aqs done with \- it runs
\f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R], frees that destructor\(aqs trampoline, then
releases the underlying allocation and nulls the pointer (see
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R]).
.PP
Usage:
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) ();
.EE
.PP
With parameters:
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) (42, \(dqtest\(dq);
.EE
.PP
Because the object lives on the heap, it can outlive the scope it was
created in and be returned from a function:
.IP
.EX
void    *a_function(void)
{
    new (test_object_type, result) (1);

    \f[B]return\f[R] (result);
}
.EE
.PP
Qualifiers work here too.
Note that \f[CR]const\f[R] makes the \f[I]object\f[R] read\-only, so its
fields can no longer be assigned directly and have to go through a
method:
.IP
.EX
const new (test_object_type, test) (42);

test\->set(33);
\f[I]// test\->value = 33; \-> error: assignment of member \(aqvalue\(aq in read\-only object\f[R]
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS The \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] convention
Both \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] and \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] (below) call a member
named exactly \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] on the object.
This member is \f[B]not\f[R] created automatically \- like every other
bound method, it has to be injected by the constructor with
\f[CR]object__inject\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
object o_test
{
    void    (*test)(void);
    void    (*__DESTRUCTOR__)(void);
};

void my_destructor(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__eject (this\->test); \f[I]// eject every OTHER member here\f[R]
}

void o_test(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__inject (this\->test, test);
    object__inject (this\->__DESTRUCTOR__, my_destructor);
}
.EE
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs The name is \f[B]always uppercase\f[R], exactly
\f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R], regardless of whether the object was declared
with the uppercase or lowercase macro family.
The double\-underscore, all\-caps shape is the signal that this member
is framework\-recognized and reserved: don\(aqt reuse it for anything
else, and don\(aqt rely on any other casing being recognized by
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R]/\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R].
.RE
.PP
Nothing checks that \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] was actually injected.
Calling \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] or \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] on an object whose
constructor never injected \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] jumps through an
uninitialized function pointer.
Every \f[CR]object\f[R] used with \f[CR]DELETE\f[R]/\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R]
\f[B]must\f[R] declare and inject \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R].
.PP
The destructor\(aqs own job is to eject every \f[I]other\f[R] injected
member of the object (see \f[CR]OBJECT__EJECT\f[R] below) \-
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R]/\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] only take care of the destructor
member itself and, for \f[CR]DELETE\f[R], the underlying allocation.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS DELETE
.IP
.EX
#define DELETE(OBJECT_POINTER)
#define delete(object_pointer)
.EE
.PP
Releases an object that was created \f[B]on the heap\f[R] with
\f[CR]NEW\f[R].
.PP
The argument is the object pointer itself, so from outside it is the
variable \f[CR]NEW\f[R] produced, and from inside a method it is simply
\f[CR]THIS\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
new (o_test, test) ();
\&...
delete (test);
.EE
.IP
.EX
void    free_self(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    delete (this);
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R] is a no\-op on a null pointer.
Otherwise it, in order:
.IP "1." 3
Calls \f[CR]OBJECT_POINTER\->__DESTRUCTOR__()\f[R].
.IP "2." 3
Frees the executable trampoline behind \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] itself.
.IP "3." 3
Recovers the allocator/freer pair that was stored for this object when
it was created (by \f[CR]NEW\f[R], or by
\f[CR]OBJECT__MY_ALLOCATOR\f[R]/\f[CR]OBJECT__MY_FREER\f[R], see below),
and calls it on the allocation.
.IP "4." 3
Sets \f[CR]OBJECT_POINTER\f[R] to \f[CR]0\f[R].
.PP
The destructor is still responsible for ejecting every \f[B]other\f[R]
injected member first \- \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] does not walk the object\(aqs
members, it only knows about \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] and the
underlying allocation:
.IP
.EX
void    my_destructor(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__eject (this\->test); \f[I]// every member except __DESTRUCTOR__ itself\f[R]
}

void    free_self(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    delete (this); \f[I]// runs __DESTRUCTOR__ (ejects \(gatest\(ga), then frees\f[R]
}
.EE
.PP
Because step 2 and the pointer\-null happen automatically, nothing needs
to be ejected or nulled by hand for the destructor member or the
allocation itself \- only the object\(aqs \f[I]other\f[R] bound methods
need an explicit \f[CR]object__eject\f[R] inside the destructor.
.PP
After the call the pointer is \f[CR]0\f[R]; nothing in the object may be
touched again.
.PP
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R] is heap\-only.
Calling it on a stack object (created with \f[CR]obj\f[R]) is undefined
\- use \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] for those instead.
.PP
If the pointer was declared \f[CR]const\f[R], delete it from inside a
method, where the \f[CR]this\f[R] produced by \f[CR]OBJECT__CONNECT\f[R]
carries no qualifier, or cast it at the call site \- depending on how
strict the compiler is about qualifiers on \f[CR]free\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS DESTROY
.IP
.EX
#define DESTROY(THE_OBJECT)
#define destroy(the_object)
.EE
.PP
Runs the destructor of an object that lives \f[B]on the stack\f[R]
(created with \f[CR]OBJ\f[R]/\f[CR]obj\f[R]).
It calls \f[CR]THE_OBJECT.__DESTRUCTOR__()\f[R] and frees that
destructor\(aqs own trampoline \- nothing else.
.IP
.EX
obj (o_test, test) ();
\&...
destroy (test);
.EE
.PP
Unlike \f[CR]DELETE\f[R], \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] does not check for null,
does not touch any allocation, and does not null out its argument \-
there is no allocation to release for a stack object, and the variable
itself goes out of scope normally at the end of the block.
As with \f[CR]DELETE\f[R], the destructor function is still responsible
for ejecting every other injected member:
.IP
.EX
void    my_destructor(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__eject (this\->test);
}

obj (o_test, test) ();
\&...
destroy (test); \f[I]// runs __DESTRUCTOR__, frees its trampoline\f[R]
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] does not distinguish stack objects from heap objects
by itself \- calling it on a heap pointer runs the destructor and frees
its trampoline but leaks the object allocation, since only
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R] knows how to give that back.
Use \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] for stack objects and \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] for heap
objects.
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__CONNECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__CONNECT(OBJECT_TYPE_NAME)
#define object__connect(object_type_name)
.EE
.PP
Connects a function to an object instance.
.PP
This macro defines a pointer named \f[CR]THIS\f[R] (or \f[CR]this\f[R]
in the lowercase form) that refers to the parent object.
It reads the object address straight out of the register the trampoline
placed it in, so it must be the \f[B]first statement\f[R] of the
function.
.PP
\f[B]Examples\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
void test1(int a)
{
    OBJECT__CONNECT (o_object);

    THIS\->value = a;
}

void test2(int a)
{
    object__connect (o_object);

    this\->value = a;
}
.EE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__MY_ALLOCATOR / OBJECT__MY_FREER
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__MY_ALLOCATOR(ALLOCATOR)
#define OBJECT__MY_FREER(FREER)
.EE
.PP
By default, a heap object created with \f[CR]NEW\f[R] is allocated with
\f[CR]ALLOC\f[R] and released with \f[CR]FREE\f[R].
\f[CR]OBJECT__MY_ALLOCATOR\f[R]/\f[CR]OBJECT__MY_FREER\f[R] let a
constructor override that pair for its own object type \- for example,
to use a pool allocator, a tracking allocator, or anything else with the
same signature as \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R]/\f[CR]FREE\f[R].
.PP
Both go where \f[CR]OBJECT__CONNECT\f[R] would otherwise go, as the
\f[B]first statement\f[R] of the constructor, and both are needed
together \- \f[CR]MY_ALLOCATOR\f[R] performs the allocation but does
\f[B]not\f[R] by itself record what to call to free it;
\f[CR]MY_FREER\f[R] records that:
.IP
.EX
void o_test(void)
{
    OBJECT__MY_ALLOCATOR (my_alloc);
    OBJECT__MY_FREER (my_free);

    OBJECT__CONNECT (o_test);

    ...
}
.EE
.PP
\f[CR]ALLOCATOR\f[R] must accept a single \f[CR]LET\f[R] size and return
a \f[CR]PTR\f[R], exactly like \f[CR]ALLOC\f[R].
\f[CR]FREER\f[R] must accept a single \f[CR]PTR\f[R] and return
\f[CR]void\f[R], exactly like \f[CR]FREE\f[R]; it is what
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R] will call on the allocation later.
.RS
.PP
/!\(rs \f[B]Always pair these.\f[R] \f[CR]OBJECT__MY_ALLOCATOR\f[R] on
its own leaves the allocation\(aqs stored free\-function uninitialized
\- \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] will read garbage there and call it, which is
undefined behavior.
There is no compiler check for \(dqthe constructor called the allocator
but forgot the freer\(dq: it is the constructor author\(aqs
responsibility to always call both, in that order, every time the object
type is constructed.
.PP
If a constructor only needs a custom allocator but the \f[I]default\f[R]
freer (\f[CR]FREE\f[R]) is still correct for it, call
\f[CR]OBJECT__MY_FREER (FREE)\f[R] explicitly rather than assuming the
slot defaults to it.
.RE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__INJECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__INJECT(TARGET, SOURCE)
#define object__inject(target, source)
.EE
.PP
Used to inject a function into an object instance.
.PP
Functions are \f[B]not\f[R] automatically bound; injection must be done
manually, usually inside the constructor.
.PP
\f[CR]TARGET\f[R] is the member that will hold the bound method,
\f[CR]SOURCE\f[R] is the plain function that implements it.
Both names are given explicitly, so the member and the function do not
have to share a name:
.IP
.EX
void o_object(int a)
{
    object__connect (o_object);

    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->test, test); \f[I]// this\->test  = test\f[R]
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->abc, test); \f[I]// this\->abc = test\f[R]
    object__inject (struct_ptr\->abc, test); \f[I]// any lvalue works\f[R]

    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value = a;
}
.EE
.PP
Under the hood the macro assembles a small trampoline that loads the
object address into a register and jumps to \f[CR]SOURCE\f[R], then
copies it into executable memory with \f[CR]ALLOC_EXE\f[R].
That trampoline is what makes \f[CR]OBJECT__CONNECT\f[R] able to recover
\f[CR]this\f[R], and it is why every injected member owns a small
executable allocation that has to be released again with
\f[CR]OBJECT__EJECT\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__EJECT
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__EJECT(MEMBER_NAME)
#define object__eject(member_name)
.EE
.PP
Releases the executable trampoline behind an injected member.
Every bound method injected with \f[CR]OBJECT__INJECT\f[R] owns one such
allocation, and it has to be given back explicitly \- nothing does this
automatically except \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R]\(aqs own trampoline,
which \f[CR]DELETE\f[R]/\f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] free by themselves.
.PP
The natural place to eject every \f[I]other\f[R] member is the
object\(aqs own \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R] function, so a single call to
\f[CR]delete\f[R]/\f[CR]destroy\f[R] cleans up the whole object:
.IP
.EX
object o_test
{
    void    (*test)(void);
    void    (*__DESTRUCTOR__)(void);
};

void test(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);
}

void my_destructor(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->test); \f[I]// every member except __DESTRUCTOR__ itself\f[R]
}

void o_test(void)
{
    object__connect (o_test);

    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->test, test);
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->__DESTRUCTOR__, my_destructor);
}
.EE
.PP
Do \f[B]not\f[R] eject \f[CR]__DESTRUCTOR__\f[R]\(aqs own member from
inside itself \- \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] and \f[CR]DESTROY\f[R] free that
trampoline right after calling it, so ejecting it manually as well would
double\-free it.
.PP
For objects created with \f[CR]new\f[R], ejecting only gives back the
executable memory of the injected methods.
The object allocation itself is released by \f[CR]DELETE\f[R] (see
\f[CR]DELETE\f[R]), not by \f[CR]OBJECT__EJECT\f[R].
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__INITIALIZE
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__INITIALIZE(VARIABLE, TYPE)
#define object__initialize(variable, type)
.EE
.PP
Used to initialize or modify a variable that is normally declared as
\f[CR]const\f[R], while keeping the variable read\-only to normal user
code.
.PP
\f[CR]VARIABLE\f[R] is the variable or object member to initialize, and
\f[CR]TYPE\f[R] is its declared type:
.IP
.EX
object test_object_type
{
    void        (*add)(int);
    const int   value;
    void        (*free)(void);
};

void test_object_type(int start_var)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    object__initialize (\f[B]this\f[R]\->value, int) = start_var;
}
.EE
.PP
Once initialized, \f[CR]value\f[R] can be read normally:
.IP
.EX
printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test.value);
.EE
.PP
But attempting to modify it directly is rejected by the compiler:
.IP
.EX
test.value = 33; \f[I]// Error: value is const\f[R]
.EE
.PP
When modification is required internally, \f[CR]object__initialize\f[R]
can be used again to access the underlying storage:
.IP
.EX
object__initialize (test.value, int) = 33;
.EE
.PP
The macro works by taking the address of \f[CR]VARIABLE\f[R], casting
that address to a pointer to \f[CR]TYPE\f[R], and dereferencing it:
.IP
.EX
*(TYPE *)(&VARIABLE)
.EE
.PP
This allows the object implementation to write to storage declared as
\f[CR]const\f[R] without removing the \f[CR]const\f[R] qualifier from
the public object interface.
.PP
The intended use is for object members that should be \f[B]readable by
the user but only writable by the object implementation\f[R], such as
internal state, configuration values, or constructor\-initialized
properties.
.PP
For example:
.IP
.EX
object test_object_type
{
    const int value;
};

void test_object_type(int start_var)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    object__initialize (\f[B]this\f[R]\->value, int) = start_var;
}
.EE
.PP
The user can then read the value:
.IP
.EX
printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test.value);
.EE
.PP
while direct modification is prevented:
.IP
.EX
test.value = 33; \f[I]// Error\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The implementation can still explicitly initialize or update the value:
.IP
.EX
object__initialize (test.value, int) = 33;
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[CR]OBJECT__INITIALIZE\f[R] and \f[CR]object__initialize\f[R] are
equivalent forms.
The uppercase form follows the macro naming convention, while the
lowercase form is intended for normal object\-oriented usage.
.RE
.PP
   *   *   *   *   *
.SS OBJECT__COPY
.IP
.EX
#define OBJECT__COPY(DST, SRC)
#define object__copy(dst, src)
.EE
.PP
It copies your object to destination.
.PP
You can also use memcpy for that instead of this macro.
.PP
And be aware: this is not creating a new object!!!
.PP
Example:
.IP
.EX
object o_object test;
obj (o_object, test2) ();

object__copy (&test, &test2);
\f[I]// Now both test and test2 are same\f[R]
.EE
.SS Full Example
.IP
.EX
#define INCL_CMT_OBJECT \f[I]// DEFINE OOP\f[R]
#include \f[B]\(dqCMT/CMT.H\(dq\f[R]
#include \f[B]<stdio.h>\f[R]

object test_object_type
{
    void    (*add)(int);
    void    (*set)(int);
    int     value;
    void    (*__DESTRUCTOR__)(void);
};

void    add(int value)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value += value;
}

void    set(int value)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value = value;
}

void    my_destructor(void)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->add);
    object__eject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->set);
    \f[I]// __DESTRUCTOR__\(aqs own trampoline is freed automatically\f[R]
    \f[I]// by DELETE/DESTROY \- don\(aqt eject it here yourself\f[R]
}

void    test_object_type(int start_var)
{
    object__connect (test_object_type);

    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->add, add);
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->set, set);
    object__inject (\f[B]this\f[R]\->__DESTRUCTOR__, my_destructor);
    \f[B]this\f[R]\->value = start_var;
}

void    *a_function(void)
{
    \f[B]new\f[R] (test_object_type, result) (1); \f[I]// Heap\f[R]

    \f[B]return\f[R] (result);
}

int main(void)
{
    object test_object_type *a;

    \f[I]// Yeah, variable attributes works too\f[R]
    const \f[B]new\f[R] (test_object_type, test_h) (42);
    obj (test_object_type, test_s) (22); \f[I]// Stack\f[R]

    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_h\->value); \f[I]// 42\f[R]
    test_h\->add(42);
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_h\->value); \f[I]// 84\f[R]
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_s.value); \f[I]// 22\f[R]

    test_s.value = 33;
    test_h\->set(33); \f[I]// I needed to do this in C++ way.\f[R]
    \f[I]// test_h\->value = 33;\f[R]
    \f[I]// error: assignment of member \(aqvalue\(aq in read\-only object\f[R]

    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_h\->value); \f[I]// 33\f[R]
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, test_s.value); \f[I]// 33\f[R]

    \f[B]delete\f[R] (test_h); \f[I]// Heap: runs __DESTRUCTOR__, then frees the allocation\f[R]
    destroy (test_s); \f[I]// Stack: runs __DESTRUCTOR__ only, nothing to free\f[R]

    a = a_function();
    printf(\(dq%d\(rsn\(dq, a\->value); \f[I]// 1\f[R]
    \f[B]delete\f[R] (a);
    \f[B]return\f[R] (0);
}
.EE
.SS Notes
Trampolines are currently generated for \f[B]Intel\f[R] CPUs in 16, 32
and 64\-bit mode.
ARM and PowerPC are work in progress.
.PP
The header suppresses the stack protector around its own code, because
GCC\(aqs canary prologue clobbers the register the trampoline delivers
the object pointer in.
The suppression is pushed and popped, so it does not leak into the
translation unit that included the header.
